WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:05.340
- Hi everyone, Sal Khan here.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:07.730
Welcome to the daily home room.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:10.090
For those of y'all who
don't know what this is,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:12.550
you're just showing up off
from Facebook or YouTube.

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:14.380
This is something that we started

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.860
once we started seeing
mass school closures

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.860
and it's really a way for
all of us to stay connected.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.160
Obviously Khan Academy as a not-for-profit

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.070
with a mission of providing

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:24.290
free world-class education
for anyone anywhere.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.470
Over the last many years
we've been building resources,

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:30.190
that we've saw students
use both outside of school

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:31.970
and inside of school with teachers.

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:33.840
And we could've never
foreseen this crisis,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.830
but when it started happening

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:36.180
we realized given our mission,

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.440
it's our duty to step
up in any way possible

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:41.560
to support you the student,
the teacher and the parent.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.760
And so we've been putting out webinars

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.890
to help parents and teachers get set up.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:48.040
We've been issuing daily schedules,

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:50.660
we just issued some
learning plans last week

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:52.780
and we also thought it'd be fun
to have something like this,

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:53.930
just a way to stay connected

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:55.690
and times with social distancing

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.530
and talk about interesting subjects.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:00:58.850
It might be related to Khan Academy.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.280
It might be education generally,

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:02.740
it might be just how do we make...

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:05.740
How do we navigate this crisis
that we're going through.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.930
I will give my the plug
that I give every day,

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:10.660
remind everyone that we are supported

00:01:10.660 --> 00:01:12.270
through philanthropic donations

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.520
from folks like yourself.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.990
So if you're in a position to
please think about donating,

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.330
I want to give a special shout out

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:18.910
to several corporations who've stepped up

00:01:18.910 --> 00:01:20.720
in the last few weeks.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.870
Bank of America stepped
up that first weekend

00:01:22.870 --> 00:01:23.703
when it was clear that

00:01:23.703 --> 00:01:25.170
we were gonna have the school closings,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.570
Google.org, AT&amp;T, Novartis,

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.460
if any of you represent corporations

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:30.690
please reach out to us.

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.650
We were running a deficit
even before this crisis

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:34.857
and our costs have gone up since then

00:01:34.857 --> 00:01:36.330
and there's all sorts of things

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:38.150
we wanna do and accelerate forward

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:40.000
but we're only going to be able to do it

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:41.100
if we're able to get more support.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.320
And thank you for the
many thousands of people

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.920
who've donated over the last few weeks.

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.190
It all makes a difference

00:01:46.190 --> 00:01:48.700
and allows us to accelerate our work.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:50.040
So the focus of today,

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.390
I have a very fun guest

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:52.470
who's a close friend of mine

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.040
and also does very
important work in the world.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.683
We have Jim Steyer from
Common Sense Media, hi Jim.

00:01:59.670 --> 00:02:02.090
- Hi Sal and great to be here

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.020
and by the way, you should
give money to Khan Academy

00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:08.130
it is a fabulous organization

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.380
and its tires are donors.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:10.310
We're a not-for-profit

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.740
but there's nobody better
than Sal Khan Academy.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.220
He's right about that.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:15.630
- I did not tell Jim to say that,

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.880
but that's why we are
close friends he knows.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:21.740
So first one on this conversation,

00:02:21.740 --> 00:02:22.780
I'm gonna ask you a few questions

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:24.380
about Common Sense Media generally

00:02:24.380 --> 00:02:27.300
and maybe some of the things
that we're doing together

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:28.410
and how we're navigating the crisis.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.070
But if anyone has questions,

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:31.640
feel free to go on YouTube and Facebook

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.130
and our team members
will surface questions

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.490
if you have questions for Jim

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:35.910
about his work at Common Sense Media

00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.280
or any questions for me as well.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:39.770
But Jim, maybe a good place to start.

00:02:39.770 --> 00:02:42.400
Tell folks what Common Sense Media is

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:43.553
and why you started it.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.220
Okay, so we are a nonprofit organization

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:51.100
and really the leading kids media

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.580
and advocacy organization in the U S.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.210
I started it cause I'm
a former school teacher.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:58.410
And at Stanford prof

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.870
who cares a ton about
really educating families

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.210
and kids in general

00:03:03.210 --> 00:03:04.600
but also about the impact of media

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.000
and technology on their lives.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.850
I started at 15 years ago.

00:03:07.850 --> 00:03:09.310
We do three things.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.840
We rate, educate and advocate.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.150
So for folks out there in
the audience who know us,

00:03:14.150 --> 00:03:16.848
we've made every movie
TV, video game, website,

00:03:16.848 --> 00:03:18.750
book, music, you name it

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.260
Common Sense rates it
and gives you information

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.080
so parents can make better
choices for their kids.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.670
The educate part of our work is

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:26.730
we created the field of

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.140
digital citizenship, digital literacy.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:30.170
So we have a curriculum

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:31.710
in most of the schools
in the United States

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.760
about the safe, ethical,
responsible use of media

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:39.520
and technology at home
and in the classroom.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.020
And then finally, we're a
leading child advocacy group

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.020
that advocates for everything like

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:46.950
everybody having Wi-Fi and broadband.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.980
Every student in America
having access to Khan Academy,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.320
these kinds of resources

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.540
and also on issues like
education and healthcare

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.369
and all these issues that
matter to kids and families

00:03:57.369 --> 00:03:58.202
who are in the U S

00:03:58.202 --> 00:04:00.740
so a big nonprofit just like Khan Academy

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.893
and a huge fan of Khan Academy and Sal.

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:07.270
- We're fans of you and all
of the work y'all do as well.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:09.050
And so to make it tangible
for a lot of folks,

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:10.860
I use Common Sense
Media with my own family

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:14.310
when I'm just trying to
find good stuff for them.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.990
We're doing movie nights once a week now

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.100
with all the social distancing

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:21.920
and we turn to Common
Sense Media a lot for that.

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.570
What are some of the things,

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:26.010
and I know we have some stuff
going on together as well,

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.530
but where's... what were y'all focused on

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:30.250
before this crisis hit

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:33.863
and how has that evolved
to some degree since then?

00:04:34.780 --> 00:04:36.140
- Okay, great question Sal.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:38.720
So basically before the crisis hit

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.810
we had this big consumer platform,

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:43.080
but 100 million people on it

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.480
trying to figure out what movies to watch

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.070
for their kids on movie night

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.720
or what video games were appropriate

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.570
for a 12 year old et cetera.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.510
And we were in most of
the schools in the U S

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.300
basically working with
teachers and educators saying,

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.610
here's how to use the internet.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:59.820
Here's how to use cell
phones and social media

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.590
in a really responsible way.

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:03.722
And when this crisis hit,

00:05:03.722 --> 00:05:05.720
one of the first things
we thought was like wow

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:07.700
we need to work with Khan Academy.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:09.550
But we also thought wouldn't it be great

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.080
to create one central hub

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.180
where schools could go and
where families could go

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:17.770
get fined at one click of a button.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:20.040
The best educational resources

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.370
could deal with the COVID-19 crisis.

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.550
So along with the support
of folks like Khan Academy,

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.940
we created Wide Open School.org,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.570
which is this new platform

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:34.010
our colleagues at Amplify
helped build the platform.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:37.820
And then honestly, we link to
the best content out there,

00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:41.390
Khan Academy, national geographic,

00:05:41.390 --> 00:05:43.430
Sesame street, et cetera.

00:05:43.430 --> 00:05:47.040
And the idea was make it
really easy for parents

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.620
and students who are at home

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.190
and we're doing distance learning

00:05:50.190 --> 00:05:52.970
and also for schools
around the United States

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:54.980
to have one central place

00:05:54.980 --> 00:05:57.530
where they could get
the content they wanted

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.310
and actually to do homeschooling.

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:01.070
And as you mentioned, Sal,

00:06:01.070 --> 00:06:03.390
having a daily schedule and you there

00:06:03.390 --> 00:06:06.467
is really important cause
everybody's at home right now

00:06:06.467 --> 00:06:08.410
and trying to do learning from home

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.470
and whether they're on Khan Academy

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.630
or Wide Open School, it's just how

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:14.260
we're just trying to help everybody

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.840
learn from home and we
think this is gonna be

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:18.820
changing the nature of
education long-term.

00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:22.080
Just like Khan Academy changed
the nature of education

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.773
when Sal founded Khan Academy.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.830
- Well, what's your
sense, a lot of questions.

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:28.570
I'm having a lot of
conversations these days

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:31.010
obviously about the whole virus generally,

00:06:31.010 --> 00:06:33.800
but how do you think
this is going to play out

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:37.180
over the next several weeks or months,

00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:38.670
especially in relation to school

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:41.096
and kids maybe generally speaking?

00:06:41.096 --> 00:06:44.050
- I actually think that
ultimately we're gonna listen

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:45.910
and we're here in California, Sal.

00:06:45.910 --> 00:06:48.300
So we've had a governor
really step forward

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.030
and mayors and stuff.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:51.780
I think we're gonna be sad to say,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:53.300
I think we're gonna be learning from home

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:55.280
for the rest of the school year for sure.

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:58.590
And I think that even for the rest of 2020

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:02.060
much of learning is gonna be
online much more than normal.

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:04.360
And therefore everybody out there

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.280
is gonna need to be able
to access the internet

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:10.923
is gonna be able to access
resources like Khan Academy.

00:07:11.822 --> 00:07:14.510
And so I think this is gonna
last longer than normal

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:16.890
and I think it's really
important for all of us.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:20.640
I have four children, Sal I
know you're a doubting dad to.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.041
I think we have to
explain to our kids that

00:07:22.041 --> 00:07:23.800
we're gonna be at home,

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:25.010
it's good family time,

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:27.550
but we're gonna need to
figure out how to do school.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.350
Also how to take breaks and get exercise

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.310
and not sit in front of
a screen all day long.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.940
So I think this is gonna
last longer than we think.

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:38.420
I think for example, it's
gonna probably affect summers

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:41.000
and a lot of us are gonna
probably do some more at home

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:42.900
than we were planning on.

00:07:42.900 --> 00:07:46.570
I know my kids who are a little
older than your kids Sal,

00:07:46.570 --> 00:07:50.170
they are in college and grad school

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:53.140
and they are taking their
classes at home and I...

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:55.500
their summer jobs have changed.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:58.860
So I sad to say, I think
we're gonna have to figure out

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:00.530
how to work with this
over the four months.

00:08:00.530 --> 00:08:01.700
I don't think it's just gonna be weeks.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.650
I think it'll be a few
months to be honest.

00:08:03.650 --> 00:08:05.460
Cause of the virus.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:07.830
- Yeah, and we're getting
some comments off of YouTube

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:11.090
Tuzeta Roro says, I have
been using this with my kids.

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.180
I think they're referring to

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.780
either Common Sense or Wide Open School.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:16.840
And I've been using this
with my kids this week

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:20.280
and it helps me feel less
guilty about screen time.

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:21.440
Actually that's an interesting issue.

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.030
How do you think about coping
with screen-time guilt?

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:26.250
I'm happy to give my own views on that.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.430
Although I'm conflicted.

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:31.430
What are your thoughts about
that as a parent and an expert?

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:35.190
- So I actually, I'd love to
hear what you think you Sal

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.490
as parent and an expert.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:39.350
So definitely you're gonna have
more screen time right now.

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:40.490
There's just no question

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:42.020
because literally my kids

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:43.640
are going to school online right now,

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.020
so they're going to be
in front of a screen

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:46.670
more than they are.

00:08:46.670 --> 00:08:49.900
And as Common Sense where
we're the leading spokespeople

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:52.160
on this issue about balance and moderation

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:53.110
when it comes to screen time.

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:55.500
And by the way, it's a
really important message,

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:57.680
but I think that we have to understand

00:08:57.680 --> 00:08:59.930
these are unique circumstances.

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:02.310
So I do think that we should let up on

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:03.870
some of the hard and fast rules

00:09:03.870 --> 00:09:06.380
we want our kids going to
school doing online learning.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:09.140
We want them using Khan Academy resources.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:12.230
But I think you also as
a parent, for example,

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:14.470
really wanna have balance
in your kid's life

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:16.360
and you don't wanna plunk
them in front of a screen

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:17.460
for eight hours in a row.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.430
That's my take.

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:20.319
And that's what we're trying
to do with this tire children.

00:09:20.319 --> 00:09:21.247
How about you?

00:09:21.247 --> 00:09:22.610
So what do you think?

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:24.140
- I feel very similar.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.860
I've always my answer even
before this crisis was,

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:29.620
it's not so much measuring what
is happening on the screen,

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:31.290
but it's happening with
measuring what's happening

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:33.040
in the student's whole life.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.730
And so if a student is getting time

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.810
where they're socializing,
spending time with their family,

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:38.970
they're running outside,

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:42.100
they have time for creativity, et cetera,

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:44.080
then I'm less worried
about that screen time,

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:46.800
especially if that screen time
is constructive screen time.

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:48.050
I think most of us if we saw

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.220
our middle school students spend

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.490
three hours composing a poem,

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:54.880
we wouldn't view that
as negative screen time

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.940
or if we saw them I thought I'm a big fan

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:00.370
of reading a book for real,

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:02.310
but I know a lot of people
like reading on a Kindle

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.130
that's not the end of the
world type of screen time

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:06.520
if someone wants to.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:07.540
I remember when I was a kid,

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.700
if I got really excited about some

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:11.720
I actually didn't have a
computer at home growing up,

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:13.410
but I, at the local university

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:15.670
I somehow convinced a professor

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:17.620
to give me access to the computers.

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:19.050
But there were times where I spent

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:20.910
12 hours straight, 20 hours straight,

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:23.010
just creating something, coding something.

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:26.312
And that was, I mean, it was
probably a little unhealthy.

00:10:26.312 --> 00:10:28.370
I probably had more
caffeine than I should have,

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:30.270
but it was actually
constructed screen time

00:10:30.270 --> 00:10:31.550
and obviously if you're learning.

00:10:31.550 --> 00:10:33.250
And so, yeah, I'm trying
to balance it myself.

00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:35.470
And especially in this
time of social distancing

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:36.600
and everyone is together.

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:39.080
Yesterday me and the family,

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:40.330
we were all on the couch together.

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:41.700
We had the popcorn going.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:45.130
We watched two episodes of
Will Smith's documentary

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:48.260
that's on Disney plus
around one strange rock.

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:49.850
That was great, I thought that was great.

00:10:49.850 --> 00:10:52.270
Screen time and great family time.

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:54.780
We learned a ton about
aliens and astronauts

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:58.050
and how life happened to exist on earth.

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:00.170
So I think that kind of thing
when you're with your family,

00:11:00.170 --> 00:11:02.470
we're having a good
conversation around it.

00:11:02.470 --> 00:11:04.980
It was two hours of screen
time of TV watching.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:08.130
But I felt like it was
a lot of bonding too.

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:10.880
So yeah, there's a balance.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:11.713
- I agree with you.

00:11:11.713 --> 00:11:13.070
I mean I actually think
that's what I would say.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.950
I think it's quantity and quality

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:17.350
and you're absolutely right.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:20.880
And the other thing about
this COVID-19 epidemic is

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:22.570
it forces more family time,

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:23.820
which is a really good thing.

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:26.210
I mean, your kids are a
little younger than ours.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:27.600
I know, you know, Sal.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.620
And so for me, I have
kids who are teenagers

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:32.710
and college age a little older right.

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:33.810
And it's great because

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.410
you actually are spending
more time as a family.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:38.660
You're having family dinners.

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:40.600
But I do think that there's,

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:42.180
it's when you talked about screen time,

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:43.970
you should think about quality

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.390
in terms of do good stuff.

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:47.550
Like just like you just
described family screen time,

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:48.880
but educational stuff.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:50.940
If you're gonna read a
book online, that's great.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:53.140
Not too much, just junk if you will.

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:54.490
It's sort of like food

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:56.720
and you're thinking about
your nutritional diet.

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:59.730
You don't wanna have your
kid only have junk food.

00:11:59.730 --> 00:12:01.890
But it's also taking breaks.

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:04.940
You mentioned exercising, getting outside

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:07.370
as much as possible, getting a break,

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:08.950
sort of having a boundary that way,

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:11.090
the daily schedule that
we were talking about

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:13.760
is important and it's balanced moderation.

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:16.750
But we're gonna be in front
of screens more often,

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:20.313
so let's make it constructive
quality screen time, why not?

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:23.150
- Yeah, and there's a
couple of questions we have

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:26.810
for Amy Liang on YouTube is asking,

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:30.840
what inspired you to make the
website Jim Steyer and well,

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:33.183
maybe I'll broaden the
question a little bit.

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:36.840
You pointed you used to
be a Stanford professor.

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:38.780
You're a lawyer by training.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:41.440
What did you see 15 years ago

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.800
where you said, hey, there's
something wrong with society.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.893
We need a not-for-profit,
like Common Sense Media.

00:12:47.964 --> 00:12:48.797
- I'll say the honest answer.

00:12:48.797 --> 00:12:50.890
So my life work, I've
been a Stanford professor

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:53.100
and now I'm grading
myself, but for 30 years.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:57.020
And I was a civil rights lawyer
first that maybe is correct.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:58.720
And what happened was

00:12:58.720 --> 00:12:59.590
I wanted to create

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:01.800
the most important child
advocacy group in United States.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:02.770
I'm really an advocate.

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:05.820
My life work is as an educator teacher

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:07.230
and as an advocate for children.

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:08.520
That's really what I do.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:09.840
And when we started

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:11.200
when I started Common Sense,

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.780
we created the web platform,

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:15.310
the platform and all the ratings

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:16.720
and reviews and all that stuff

00:13:16.720 --> 00:13:18.730
basically to get the public

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:20.120
to care about children.

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.970
And also we realized that that time,

00:13:21.970 --> 00:13:23.840
this is 15 years ago,

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:26.550
that movies and TV shows and music

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.730
were having a huge impact on
kids lives and family's life.

00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:31.900
Remember there was no iPhone,

00:13:31.900 --> 00:13:36.370
no Facebook, no Google,
no Snapchat, nothing.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:38.200
And in the day when we
started Common Sense,

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:39.850
it was basically movies and TV.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:42.450
And maybe some music and video games.

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.860
And but alright, the real thing for me was

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:49.287
to build a platform that
everybody could join

00:13:50.210 --> 00:13:52.150
if they wanted to care
about children generally

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:53.930
and to create this membership

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:55.760
based sort of advocacy group for children,

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:57.440
particularly for disadvantaged children

00:13:57.440 --> 00:13:58.920
who don't have a voice.

00:13:58.920 --> 00:14:01.000
And lo and behold, 15 years later

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:03.300
Oh my gosh, who knew that we would have

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.260
100 million plus users

00:14:06.206 --> 00:14:08.320
for our consumer reports guide.

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:09.580
And the truth is,

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:11.940
I didn't realize that the
media technology piece

00:14:11.940 --> 00:14:14.450
would be so big and who could have known

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:17.050
that Steve Jobs would bring out the iPhone

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.830
or that Mark Zuckerberg
would invent Facebook

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.820
or that you'd have YouTube and Snapchat

00:14:22.820 --> 00:14:25.140
and all the other things
that are so key (mumbling).

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.010
But the truth is when it happened,

00:14:27.010 --> 00:14:29.460
Common Sense was there and we did it.

00:14:29.460 --> 00:14:32.130
And so I remember when
you started Khan Academy

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:35.180
and I remember those conversations we had

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:38.323
and I was so happy that you
decided to make Khan Academy

00:14:38.323 --> 00:14:41.020
a not-for-profit because
you could have built

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:43.800
a successful for profit business,

00:14:43.800 --> 00:14:45.380
but your heart was so much in the right.

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:46.260
I never forget that

00:14:46.260 --> 00:14:48.170
your heart was so much in the right place

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:50.210
of doing this as a not-for-profit

00:14:50.210 --> 00:14:52.880
and making it the school
for everybody in the world

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:56.120
and you should be very proud
of that my friend, I'm serious.

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.210
But that's why we love
working with you guys,

00:14:58.210 --> 00:15:00.930
but I could have never
predicted 15 years ago

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:02.700
that Common Sense would
have gotten this big,

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:04.800
but the same mission is
about kids basically.

00:15:04.800 --> 00:15:06.327
It's how do you change the world for kids?

00:15:06.327 --> 00:15:09.320
How do you improve the world for kids

00:15:09.320 --> 00:15:10.690
particularly the ones
who need it the most.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:11.810
That's my biggest concern

00:15:11.810 --> 00:15:14.020
by the way during the COVID-19 crisis.

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:17.940
Is how do we take care of
the kids who are not living,

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:20.704
who are in a two room
home with five people

00:15:20.704 --> 00:15:23.780
and whose school isn't
even doing learning yet

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:26.600
because they don't even
have a system set up.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.860
So I think for those of
us, we're so fortunate,

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:31.410
I think it's really
important to remember that

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:33.770
there's like tens of
millions of kids out there

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:35.590
who have very few resources

00:15:35.590 --> 00:15:36.730
and we want to care about them

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:38.670
and give them the same opportunities

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:42.320
that Sal Khan kids and
Jim Steyer kids have.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:43.330
But that's what motivates me.

00:15:43.330 --> 00:15:44.550
That's why I started Common Sense.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.190
And like you who knew Khan
Academy would turn it,

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:49.880
you were just making videos
for your nieces and nephews.

00:15:49.880 --> 00:15:51.194
Look what happened.

00:15:51.194 --> 00:15:53.244
Sort of the same thing with Common Sense.

00:15:54.216 --> 00:15:55.050
- No, you never know.

00:15:55.050 --> 00:15:57.170
And there's a question here
from Facebook, Scott Yang,

00:15:57.170 --> 00:15:59.400
who's been consistently
asking good questions.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:00.710
I mean I'm gonna broaden his question.

00:16:00.710 --> 00:16:02.700
His question is, are video games fine?

00:16:02.700 --> 00:16:04.020
But I'm gonna broaden it, which is

00:16:04.020 --> 00:16:09.020
obviously Common Sense you
guys are rating, what is good?

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.700
What's appropriate for different ages.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:12.200
And I think all of us have a sense,

00:16:12.200 --> 00:16:14.410
you see a video game where
you're shooting people up

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:16.370
like hey, that might not be right.

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:18.080
I mean, frankly even for me myself,

00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:19.470
if I watch a movie that's,

00:16:19.470 --> 00:16:21.130
or a video game that's really gruesome,

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:23.650
I'm like this, when I was
a teenager for some reason

00:16:23.650 --> 00:16:24.483
I didn't mind it.

00:16:24.483 --> 00:16:25.790
But now when I look at it, I don't know,

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:27.420
I'm like, Oh, that's troubling.

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:29.293
I'm not sure if I can sleep tonight.

00:16:30.740 --> 00:16:32.540
And then there's things like social media

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:33.680
and things that aren't gruesome

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:35.963
but have other dynamics.

00:16:37.090 --> 00:16:38.420
There's this stuff we know intuitively,

00:16:38.420 --> 00:16:39.260
but what do we...

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:41.230
what evidence do we have that it is,

00:16:41.230 --> 00:16:43.330
maybe some of this stuff could cause harm.

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:46.200
- We know it's really a good question.

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:48.380
So video games have been a big issue

00:16:48.380 --> 00:16:49.290
for Common sense, right.

00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:50.790
From day one, right.

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:53.360
So we were the first people
we started rating them

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:55.600
and really calling out the violence

00:16:55.600 --> 00:16:57.453
in certain that the old video games.

00:16:59.309 --> 00:17:00.142
And explaining to parents

00:17:00.142 --> 00:17:02.470
and educators that too much violence

00:17:02.470 --> 00:17:03.480
is really bad for kids

00:17:03.480 --> 00:17:05.170
that there's copycat violence and there's,

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:07.480
and also you can become a near devout.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:09.520
So I would say it's really interesting

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:11.160
we because we realized

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.080
how incredibly popular video games are

00:17:13.080 --> 00:17:14.700
and they were made to be that.

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.750
I have a 16 year old son

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:18.160
who if we allowed him to

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:21.410
he would play video
games for 18 hours a day

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:23.340
and not all of them
would be the video games

00:17:23.340 --> 00:17:24.720
that I might recommend.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:26.730
And so I think a couple of things

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:30.040
we worked very closely with
the video game industry

00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:32.430
could tell about you have
to limit the violence.

00:17:32.430 --> 00:17:35.410
Also there used to be
racially stereotypical stuff

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:36.660
and a lot of sexist stuff.

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:38.660
There was a lot of content in video games.

00:17:38.660 --> 00:17:40.370
It was certainly inappropriate

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:42.000
for an eight year old or a 10 year old.

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:43.020
And then there was the issue of

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:44.920
too much time in front of a screen.

00:17:44.920 --> 00:17:49.350
So we have done a lot of advocacy
around the issue of video.

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:52.550
We've done a lot to educate
parents and teachers

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:54.420
about how much time kids love video

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:56.940
how much time kids are
spending playing video games

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.080
and also how to find the good ones.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:00.330
Because as you know Sal,

00:18:00.330 --> 00:18:02.810
there are a ton of great games out there

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:04.210
and there are different kinds of learning.

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:06.449
Some of the most creative
educational products

00:18:06.449 --> 00:18:09.060
are actually game based products

00:18:09.060 --> 00:18:10.720
that are really educational.

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:12.920
So again, I think it's both

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:15.900
quality of the game and the amount.

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:18.320
So I wouldn't let Jesse,

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:20.650
our son play 10 hours of video games

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:22.470
even though we probably would like to.

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:25.570
And you're right,
teenagers particularly boys

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:27.660
are the ones who would spend all day

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:29.140
in front of a video game machine

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:30.270
if we let them.

00:18:30.270 --> 00:18:31.640
But it's a great question

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:33.910
and I think it's one of the
reasons why Common Sense.org

00:18:33.910 --> 00:18:35.540
is such a good resource
cause you can go there

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:37.397
and check on anything and
say, hey, is this okay

00:18:37.397 --> 00:18:40.390
for my eight year old or
old or my 15 year old.

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:43.580
And, it's balanced, it's moderation.

00:18:43.580 --> 00:18:45.650
But video games can also be awesome.

00:18:45.650 --> 00:18:48.430
And I like to play them too.

00:18:48.430 --> 00:18:50.630
But I'm not nearly as
talented as my son is.

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:53.950
- And what about social media?

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:55.100
I hear at least anecdotally,

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:56.650
I've talked to university presidents,

00:18:56.650 --> 00:18:58.600
they're talking about how anxiety

00:18:58.600 --> 00:18:59.530
has gotten through the roof

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:00.950
and some people have pointed it

00:19:00.950 --> 00:19:02.050
to this as the generation

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:04.080
that kind of grew up in on social media.

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:05.580
What do y'all know about that?

00:19:06.760 --> 00:19:08.110
- Well, that's a really big issue.

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:09.620
So, first of all,

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:12.180
this is the first real
generation of digital natives

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.820
who are growing up on
social media platforms.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:17.950
And so I think we try to take
a fairly balanced approach.

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:19.420
We have been critical

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:21.760
of some of the big social media players

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:24.830
for allowing inappropriate
content on their platform.

00:19:24.830 --> 00:19:26.650
Whether it's cyber bullying

00:19:26.650 --> 00:19:29.880
or the one thing about
some of the platforms,

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:31.940
if you think about, say
Instagram and Snapchat,

00:19:31.940 --> 00:19:34.500
which are where a lot of kids are today.

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:36.990
There's a constant comparison of yourself.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:39.570
I wrote a book back in 2012

00:19:39.570 --> 00:19:41.660
called Talking Back to Facebook

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:42.559
where I talked about the fact

00:19:42.559 --> 00:19:45.180
and as the father of two daughters,

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:47.640
that millions of girls out there

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:49.756
and boys too Photoshop their images

00:19:49.756 --> 00:19:52.750
on Facebook and Instagram and Snapchat,

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:55.340
meaning they're wanting to
show an ideal personality

00:19:55.340 --> 00:19:58.000
or an ideal image, which
is just unrealistic.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:20:01.850
So we've tried to work
closely with the big platforms

00:20:01.850 --> 00:20:03.250
and tried to get them to clean up

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:04.310
some of the stuff that's on it

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:07.240
and be aware of how many
hundreds of millions of kids

00:20:07.240 --> 00:20:08.760
are on those platforms.

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:10.540
But the truth is this,

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:12.150
I know as a father of teenagers,

00:20:12.150 --> 00:20:14.070
kids are on those platforms.

00:20:14.070 --> 00:20:17.840
The question is how do you
both limit the amount of time

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:21.340
and also use those platforms
for the good stuff.

00:20:21.340 --> 00:20:23.970
And I think this is an, as a parent,

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.220
you really have to talk with your kids

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.530
about social media and teachers do too

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:29.680
much to the curriculum

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:31.540
that we have in schools
around the United States

00:20:31.540 --> 00:20:34.890
and globally is about how do
you deal with social media,

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:36.773
how do you use it responsibly?

00:20:37.610 --> 00:20:40.350
And how do you not engage in bullying

00:20:40.350 --> 00:20:42.280
or all the other kinds of things

00:20:42.280 --> 00:20:44.850
that can be negative on
a social media platform.

00:20:44.850 --> 00:20:45.940
And we're very frank

00:20:45.940 --> 00:20:48.290
with the guys who run the platforms too.

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:50.840
About trying to clean up those platforms

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:52.410
and make them kid friendly

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:53.460
and appropriate too.

00:20:53.460 --> 00:20:56.650
So that I think that the
big Common Sense mission.

00:20:56.650 --> 00:20:58.380
- Have you found that folks have been

00:20:58.380 --> 00:21:01.853
receptive to that, to your feedback?

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.040
- It depends, I will be I honestly Sal

00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:06.410
I really will say this.

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.300
It depends on the
platform and the company.

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:10.030
I think a lot of it has to do with

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:12.160
who the people who lead the organization.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:14.180
It's the same thing in a way.

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:15.410
At the end of the day,

00:21:15.410 --> 00:21:16.420
there are a ton of great people

00:21:16.420 --> 00:21:17.390
who work at Khan Academy,

00:21:17.390 --> 00:21:20.020
but you set the tone
and the culture you do.

00:21:20.020 --> 00:21:22.480
And there's a degree to which
I do that at Common Sense.

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:24.390
So I think when it comes to that,

00:21:24.390 --> 00:21:25.900
it really depends platform by

00:21:25.900 --> 00:21:27.600
I think that's really true in life

00:21:28.577 --> 00:21:29.880
that you have to show leadership

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:32.410
just like as a parent
and you and your spouse

00:21:32.410 --> 00:21:34.900
have to set the family role models

00:21:34.900 --> 00:21:37.310
and you have to set good
examples for your kids,

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:39.370
including social media and technology.

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:41.263
But I think that some of the platforms

00:21:41.263 --> 00:21:43.698
have been more responsive than others.

00:21:43.698 --> 00:21:45.490
The other thing is, it's funny

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:46.630
cause as a Stanford professor,

00:21:46.630 --> 00:21:49.820
I've had so many thousands of students

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:52.460
who went into the technology industry.

00:21:52.460 --> 00:21:54.810
I think that people who
work in the tech industry

00:21:54.810 --> 00:21:56.560
have to realize the incredible impact

00:21:56.560 --> 00:21:58.110
of the platforms they built

00:21:58.110 --> 00:22:00.290
on the lives of everybody out there.

00:22:00.290 --> 00:22:01.750
And they have to be responsible

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:03.740
for the content of those platforms

00:22:03.740 --> 00:22:06.360
and also some of the
design of those platforms.

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:08.780
So that you don't have
people sitting there

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:13.780
watching nine hours in a
row of their favorite show.

00:22:13.780 --> 00:22:15.760
So I think it's really about,

00:22:15.760 --> 00:22:16.883
I think that there's a big role

00:22:16.883 --> 00:22:19.460
for Common Sense and folks like you, Sal

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:21.920
and all of us and parents
and kids out there

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.350
to ask the platforms to be responsible.

00:22:24.350 --> 00:22:27.370
And I think one of the
things about this crisis

00:22:27.370 --> 00:22:30.120
is a lot of them have really
stepped up to the plate.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:32.550
And if you look at the
partners with Wide Open School,

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:34.210
haven't just been you guys,

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:37.710
but Google and zoom have been really good

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:39.320
and Apple and that's been great

00:22:39.320 --> 00:22:43.380
because they have such
huge reach and they owe it,

00:22:43.380 --> 00:22:45.030
I really feel they have a responsibility

00:22:45.030 --> 00:22:47.270
to the public to do right by the public.

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.060
And I think that in a number of cases

00:22:49.060 --> 00:22:50.947
they're really stepping up and should be.

00:22:50.947 --> 00:22:52.960
And they're gonna need
to do that long term

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:55.560
as education becomes increasingly online

00:22:55.560 --> 00:22:58.750
and learn at home distance
learning kind of stuff.

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:00.620
- Yeah and in fairness to them, and it is,

00:23:00.620 --> 00:23:01.920
these are incredibly hard issues

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:03.060
and obviously we are right now

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:04.450
streaming on YouTube, Facebook.

00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:06.520
So we're able to leverage social media

00:23:06.520 --> 00:23:09.250
for hopefully not evil.

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:13.770
And even some of the things
that are not optimal about it,

00:23:13.770 --> 00:23:17.610
they're all these really
interesting edge cases

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:20.730
around free speech or how
do you control things?

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:22.490
Or if you have some
machine learning algorithm

00:23:22.490 --> 00:23:24.700
that's optimizing for views,

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:25.980
how do you control that?

00:23:25.980 --> 00:23:29.030
So I definitely, understand where

00:23:29.030 --> 00:23:31.240
some of their difficulties lie.

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:33.194
But it's a real thing.

00:23:33.194 --> 00:23:35.228
When we talk to university,

00:23:35.228 --> 00:23:38.210
people running universities
and high schools right now,

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:40.790
the amount of anxiety
and depression from kids,

00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:42.810
to your point, comparing
themselves to others

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:45.740
or getting bullied or bullying others is,

00:23:45.740 --> 00:23:47.490
it seems a little bit out of control.

